This rule enforces best practices for performance optimization in Rust code.

## Rule Details

- **Pattern**: `*.rs`
- **Severity**: Warning
- **Category**: Performance

## Checks

1. **Allocator Selection**
   - Applications should consider `mimalloc` as global allocator
   - Typically yields up to 25% benchmark improvements
   - Minimal code changes required
   - Profile before and after to verify gains

2. **Hot Path Identification**
   - Identify performance-relevant code paths early
   - Create benchmarks using `criterion` or `divan`
   - Regularly profile with CPU/allocation insights
   - Document performance-sensitive areas
   - Enable debug symbols: `[profile.bench] debug = 1`

3. **Common Performance Issues**
   - Frequent reallocations (growing strings/vecs without capacity)
   - Short-lived allocations
   - Memory copy overhead from unnecessary cloning
   - Repeated hashing
   - Inappropriate hasher choices (consider `ahash` or `rustc-hash`)
   - String handling alone can yield ~15% gains when optimized

4. **Throughput Optimization**
   - Design for items-per-CPU-cycle
   - Partition work into chunks for parallelism
   - Let threads handle slices independently
   - Batch operations where possible
   - Exploit CPU caches (process data linearly)
   - Yield within long async tasks

5. **Memory Management**
   - Use stack allocation when possible
   - Pre-allocate with `with_capacity()` when size is known
   - Avoid unnecessary heap allocations
   - Implement memory reuse patterns
   - Use appropriate collection types

6. **Zero-Cost Abstractions**
   - Leverage compile-time optimizations
   - Use generics for zero-cost abstractions
   - Avoid runtime overhead in hot paths
   - Use const generics where appropriate

## Examples

### Good
```rust
// Good: Using mimalloc allocator
use mimalloc::MiMalloc;

#[global_allocator]
static GLOBAL: MiMalloc = MiMalloc;

// Good: Pre-allocating capacity
fn process_items(items: &[Item]) -> Vec<ProcessedItem> {
    let mut results = Vec::with_capacity(items.len());
    for item in items {
        results.push(process(item));
    }
    results
}

// Good: Reusing buffers
struct Processor {
    buffer: Vec<u8>,
}

impl Processor {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            buffer: Vec::with_capacity(4096),
        }
    }

    pub fn process(&mut self, data: &[u8]) -> &[u8] {
        self.buffer.clear();  // Reuse allocation, don't reallocate
        self.buffer.extend_from_slice(data);
        // ... process in place ...
        &self.buffer
    }
}

// Good: Efficient iterator usage (no intermediate collection)
fn sum_positive_squares(numbers: &[i32]) -> i64 {
    numbers
        .iter()
        .filter(|&&x| x > 0)
        .map(|&x| (x as i64) * (x as i64))
        .sum()
}

// Good: Avoiding repeated hashing
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::BuildHasherDefault;
use rustc_hash::FxHasher;

type FxHashMap<K, V> = HashMap<K, V, BuildHasherDefault<FxHasher>>;

fn count_words(text: &str) -> FxHashMap<&str, usize> {
    let mut counts = FxHashMap::default();
    for word in text.split_whitespace() {
        *counts.entry(word).or_default() += 1;
    }
    counts
}

// Good: Batch processing with chunking
fn process_large_dataset(data: &[u8]) -> Vec<Result> {
    use rayon::prelude::*;

    data.par_chunks(1024)
        .map(|chunk| process_chunk(chunk))
        .collect()
}

// Good: Cache-friendly linear access
fn sum_matrix_rows(matrix: &[Vec<f64>]) -> Vec<f64> {
    matrix.iter()
        .map(|row| row.iter().sum())
        .collect()
}

// Bad: Cache-unfriendly column access
fn sum_matrix_cols_bad(matrix: &[Vec<f64>]) -> Vec<f64> {
    let cols = matrix[0].len();
    (0..cols)
        .map(|col| matrix.iter().map(|row| row[col]).sum())
        .collect()
}

// Good: Zero-cost abstraction with generics
fn process<T: AsRef<str>>(input: T) -> usize {
    input.as_ref().len()  // No runtime overhead
}

// Good: Using Entry API to avoid double lookup
fn increment_or_insert(map: &mut HashMap<String, u32>, key: &str) {
    *map.entry(key.to_string()).or_insert(0) += 1;
}

// Bad: Double lookup
fn increment_or_insert_bad(map: &mut HashMap<String, u32>, key: &str) {
    if map.contains_key(key) {
        *map.get_mut(key).unwrap() += 1;
    } else {
        map.insert(key.to_string(), 1);
    }
}

// Good: Cow for conditional ownership
use std::borrow::Cow;

fn process_text(text: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {
    if text.contains("replace_me") {
        Cow::Owned(text.replace("replace_me", "replaced"))
    } else {
        Cow::Borrowed(text)  // No allocation if no replacement needed
    }
}

// Good: Yield in long async operations
async fn process_many_items(items: Vec<Item>) -> Vec<Result> {
    let mut results = Vec::with_capacity(items.len());

    for (i, item) in items.into_iter().enumerate() {
        if i % 100 == 0 {
            tokio::task::yield_now().await;  // Cooperative yielding
        }
        results.push(process_item(item));
    }

    results
}
```

### Bad
```rust
// Bad: No capacity pre-allocation
let mut vec = Vec::new();
for i in 0..10000 {
    vec.push(i.to_string());  // Many reallocations
}

// Good:
let mut vec = Vec::with_capacity(10000);
for i in 0..10000 {
    vec.push(i.to_string());
}

// Bad: Unnecessary intermediate collection
let result: Vec<_> = numbers
    .iter()
    .filter(|&x| x > &0)
    .collect();  // Unnecessary allocation
let sum: i32 = result.iter().sum();

// Good: Chain iterators
let sum: i32 = numbers
    .iter()
    .filter(|&x| x > &0)
    .sum();

// Bad: Runtime overhead in hot path
fn process_dynamic(input: &dyn AsRef<str>) {
    let s = input.as_ref();  // Virtual dispatch in hot path
}

// Good: Static dispatch
fn process_static<T: AsRef<str>>(input: T) {
    let s = input.as_ref();  // Monomorphized, no dispatch
}

// Bad: Unnecessary cloning
fn process_data(data: Vec<String>) {
    for item in data.clone() {  // Clone entire vec?
        println!("{}", item);
    }
}

// Good: Borrow instead
fn process_data(data: &[String]) {
    for item in data {
        println!("{}", item);
    }
}

// Bad: Hot spinning
async fn wait_bad(flag: &AtomicBool) {
    while !flag.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
        // Spinning wastes CPU
    }
}

// Good: Yield or use async primitives
async fn wait_good(flag: &AtomicBool) {
    while !flag.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
        tokio::task::yield_now().await;
    }
}
```

## Benchmarking Setup

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[dev-dependencies]
criterion = { version = "0.5", features = ["html_reports"] }

[[bench]]
name = "my_benchmark"
harness = false

[profile.bench]
debug = 1  # Enable debug symbols for profiling
```

```rust
// benches/my_benchmark.rs
use criterion::{black_box, criterion_group, criterion_main, Criterion};

fn benchmark_function(c: &mut Criterion) {
    c.bench_function("my_function", |b| {
        b.iter(|| {
            my_function(black_box(input))
        })
    });
}

criterion_group!(benches, benchmark_function);
criterion_main!(benches);
```

## Performance Checklist

- [ ] Profile before optimizing (don't guess)
- [ ] Consider mimalloc for ~25% improvement
- [ ] Pre-allocate collections when size is known
- [ ] Avoid intermediate collections in iterator chains
- [ ] Use appropriate hasher for HashMaps (FxHash, AHash)
- [ ] Prefer static dispatch in hot paths
- [ ] Reuse buffers instead of reallocating
- [ ] Batch operations for parallelism
- [ ] Yield in long async operations
- [ ] Enable debug symbols in bench profile

## Rationale

Performance optimization practices ensure:
- Efficient resource utilization
- Minimal runtime overhead
- Scalable applications
- Better user experience
- Lower infrastructure costs

